"# Posts Mentioning Visa or CPT Rules\n\n## CPT Rule Changes and Co-op Restrictions\n- **CPT rule change (1wbl1n6):** A student with a signed fall co-op hadn't received CPT authorization yet when an email about CPT changes said they could no longer do it. Commenters explained that authorization now covers only strictly required co-ops, \"meaning only 1 for most programs.\" One commenter said their second CPT had just been rejected [1wbl1n6].\n- **DHS/ICE memo (1vnplvc):** This post covers a UC Berkeley notice about an ICE memo that limits CPT to internships required of all students to earn the degree. Berkeley suspended \"Course Credit CPT\" applications as a result. One commenter cited Northeastern's OGS policy, which describes CPT as \"necessary and integral, or required,\" and argued the memo doesn't conflict with Northeastern's co-op system. That comment was sourced from ChatGPT [1vnplvc].\n- **Optional co-op (1x6w217):** An international undergrad asked whether they can still do a co-op if it isn't required to graduate. A commenter said that under the new rules the answer is \"most likely no.\" Others recommended asking OGS or an advisor rather than Reddit [1x6w217].\n- **London Scholars timing (1vb8bn9):** A student asked whether the F-1 CPT one-academic-year requirement would delay their co-op until fall of third year. A commenter said it depends on the assigned co-op cycle [1vb8bn9].\n- **OPT and online certificate (1wo77dg):** A student on F-1 OPT/cap-gap, with an H-1B starting October 1, was told by OGS to withdraw from an online certificate program. A commenter called CPT guidelines \"a moving target\" that are \"changing by the hour\" [1wo77dg].\n\n## F-1 Visa Process and Admissions\n- **I-20 and F-1 steps (1x0jd5l):** A newly admitted CPS student asked how to get their I-20. A reply laid out the full sequence: admission → I-20 → SEVIS fee → DS-160 → visa interview → F-1 visa → travel. It also noted that applicants must now interview in their country of nationality or residence [1x0jd5l].\n- **Visa interview questions (1wwd4sp):** A commerce graduate asked whether others with non-CS backgrounds got F-1 approval for an MSCS, and what visa officers asked [1wwd4sp].\n- **Prospective student question (1xdkyqt):** A prospective MSF student asked about the \"visa situation\" for fall 2027 [1xdkyqt].\n- **Court case (1w6pdno):** A post shared coverage of a Boston federal judge criticizing the Trump administration's changes to international student visas [1w6pdno].\n\n## UK Visa Issues (London Campus)\n- **Attendance rules (1wml31d):** London Scholars were told that attendance below about 70% could lead to visa removal. A former student said only 8 consecutive weeks below the threshold triggers removal, after a series of warnings and meetings [1wml31d].\n- **Processing delays (1w7iz8d, 1w4sepz):** Two posts describe UK student visas still pending days before departure, well past the standard 3-week processing time [1w7iz8d][1w4sepz]. In one, the student got the visa about 10 hours before their flight [1w7iz8d].\n\n## Co-op Affordability and Other Visa Mentions\n- **Co-op and affordability (1xxe8u2):** A prospective MS CS student asked whether visa/CPT constraints limit co-op options. Commenters said finding a co-op is hard for international students in the current market, and that their odds of a return offer are likely lower because of sponsorship [1xxe8u2].\n- **UHCS survey (1wwa7d4):** A survey about health services asked students with visa status about medical documentation such as RCL and leave of absence [1wwa7d4].\n\n## Note on a Keyword Match\n- **Visa the company (1xrdctk):** This post matched the keyword \"visa,\" but it refers to the payments company as a career target. It does not discuss immigration rules, although the poster does ask about recruiting and sponsorship as an international student [1xrdctk]."